    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "f45d06bd-58bd-3092-940b-c5bcdb47af9a",
                "word": "Orphism",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e2dbbd47-af1d-3329-87b7-56d38bfc09ea",
                        "entry_id": "f45d06bd-58bd-3092-940b-c5bcdb47af9a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A religious movement in antiquity, supposed to have been founded by Orpheus."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "5d637d4b-b8e1-40e8-81ed-396e9d4728f8",
                                "sense_id": "e2dbbd47-af1d-3329-87b7-56d38bfc09ea",
                                "text": "1974, The Emerson Society Quarterly, Issues 74-81, Emerson Society, page 125,\nThoreau's acquaintance with Orphism dated from at least as early as the summer of 1840, when he read Ralph Cudworth's True Intellectual System of the Universe and took notes on some of Cudworth's references to the Orphic religion."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "63ab3ffa-7718-4118-a4af-c7b134d6c95c",
                                "sense_id": "e2dbbd47-af1d-3329-87b7-56d38bfc09ea",
                                "text": "1995, Dan Urman, Paul Virgil McCracken Flesher, Ancient Synagogues: Historical Analysis and Archaeological Discovery, Part 2, E. J. Brill, page 355,\nThe oracular poems produced by Orphism and attributed to Orpheus himself, similarly reveal neither messiah nor messianic age."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8e82ac41-4357-46dd-8ef7-03ca48f07e75",
                                "sense_id": "e2dbbd47-af1d-3329-87b7-56d38bfc09ea",
                                "text": "The origins of Orphism are obscure, although they seem to date back at least to the sixth century B.C.E. Orphic ideology mainly concerned the mysteries of Dionysos, which many Greeks took very seriously."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "207aca6f-4575-462a-9edc-d3de699bd3d2",
                                "sense_id": "e2dbbd47-af1d-3329-87b7-56d38bfc09ea",
                                "text": "2010, Miguel Herrero de Jáuregui, Orphism and Christianity in Late Antiquity, De Gruyter, page 295,\nThe apologists' strategies distort the real image of Orphism, but they maintain a certain basis in reality in order to appear plausible."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ef5e867b-7b0a-39e4-8acc-d7a816244b7c",
                        "entry_id": "f45d06bd-58bd-3092-940b-c5bcdb47af9a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A minor Cubist art movement focusing on pure abstraction and bright colours."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "8b633ab8-0ce2-4c39-a7b8-b0f6d89492ae",
                                "sense_id": "ef5e867b-7b0a-39e4-8acc-d7a816244b7c",
                                "text": "“Orphism,” he writes wearily, italicizing the word for effect, “and now we speak of Orphism. Or rather, we do not speak of it. All that we've tried to say has only clouded the question.” The same day Goth's article appeared, two separate caricatures of Orphism also showed up in the popular press—one in Le rire (figure 1.26), the other in Le journal amusant[…]"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "4f0d9c10-4cce-4cea-b94c-83d6424c82af",
                        "source_entry_id": "f45d06bd-58bd-3092-940b-c5bcdb47af9a",
                        "source_sense_description": "religious movement",
                        "target_lang_word": "orphisme",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "orphisme",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8e6ea225-18e2-30dc-ae4e-aa9340003d4a",
                                        "entry_id": "1038814c-b3c5-3dd8-a330-9f97ce9c2456",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Ensemble des doctrines et des mystères orphiques."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ffaf57f3-ce07-4e1c-abb0-a5550668a927",
                                                "sense_id": "8e6ea225-18e2-30dc-ae4e-aa9340003d4a",
                                                "text": "L’orphisme, qui rappelle le poète Orphée et ses voyages, était au VIᵉ siècle avant J.C. une philosophie ayant pour corps de doctrine et d’enseignement l’immortalité de l’âme et ses pérégrinations dans des cycles de réincarnations."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "faf11f35-4543-410e-bbfb-650b125ab718",
                                                "sense_id": "8e6ea225-18e2-30dc-ae4e-aa9340003d4a",
                                                "text": "Nous pouvons ici songer à l’orphisme et au pythagorisme, dont les disciples prônent la diète sans viande."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4c3f48a0-25bf-3b1f-ba10-81fc8a7e59fc",
                                        "entry_id": "1038814c-b3c5-3dd8-a330-9f97ce9c2456",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Toute démarche intellectuelle ou processus artistique évoquant la descente aux Enfers d’Orphée."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "broadly"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f6dfbb3e-6f3d-40cd-8d2f-d4bea2d80134",
                                                "sense_id": "4c3f48a0-25bf-3b1f-ba10-81fc8a7e59fc",
                                                "text": "Aucune poésie, décidément, n’échappe à l’orphisme, dans la mesure où elle creuse sa secrète différence à l’écart des modes et des faveurs – en totale solitude ; mais aussi en constante recherche de l’autre sans quoi, sans qui « cette femme qui dort dans l’homme » (et inversement) restera perdue d’oubli ou de négation, et l’écriture se retournant sur elle-même se perdra dans ses miroitements narcissiques."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e01c3316-03b5-37f2-91e4-77d268f45398",
                                        "entry_id": "1038814c-b3c5-3dd8-a330-9f97ce9c2456",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Branche du cubisme, opposée au cubisme dit scientifique, nommée par Guillaume Apollinaire en référence à son propre poème Orphée, et qui atteindrait une abstraction pure et lyrique comparable à l’art musical."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
